this video we're going to look at Lucia  Talia's principle this particular  principle is heavily involved in our  work with dynamic equilibrium it enables  us to predict in which direction the  reversible reaction will move when we  change the conditions in the experiment  in essence it can be summarized with a  simple phrase that the equilibrium  position moves to minimize the effect of  a change in conditions  that's lechatelier's principle x' if  ever you require to quote it there are  three main ways that we can change the  conditions in experiment we can change  the temperature we can change the  pressure if we're dealing with gases or  with liquids we can change the  concentration so if we look at each of  these in turn in any reaction that we  have a plus B as an example going to a B  one direction of this reaction will be  exothermic and in this case let's say  it's the forward reaction and the other  reaction therefore will be an  endothermic reaction so we have heat  being produced in this direction and we  have heat being taken in in other words  a cooling effect in this direction so in  the case of this if we decide to  increase the temperature of the reaction  we were making this hotter  so the reactants will get hotter now to  minimize the effect of that what the  reactants will do will try to cool down  the reaction in other words to do the  opposite of making it hotter will  promote the reaction that cools it down  so if we increase the temperature in  this case we will promote the exothermic  reaction which means the dynamic  equilibrium will shift to the a plus B  side of this equation now for as of  chemists in this case that would not be  very useful because if we're doing this  reaction at all we probably want to  promote the actual product that we're  making so if we wanted to do this we  would reduce the temperature in other  words to cool it down and if we cool  down the reaction the reaction will try  to do the opposite of that  it will try to increase the temperature  which means it will promote the  exothermic reaction which is the forward  reaction so as a result if we cool down  the experiment the experiment will react  to try and increase the exothermic  reaction and therefore produce more of  our products a plus B if we start to  consider instead the number two  situation of pressure we have in the  case of and the best example to use here  is the harbor process we have a  situation where we have some nitrogen n2  plus some hydrogen h2 and it's going to  make in fact what's may remember it's a  reversible reaction and it's going to  make ammonia which is the 3 and H sorry  the 2 nh3 so  when we look at this if we look at the  reaction of these gases at this side of  the equation we have one molecule of  nitrogen but we have three molecules of  hydrogen making four molecules on this  side these four molecules exert a  pressure on the containers on the  opposite side of the reaction we just  have two ammonia molecules and those two  ammonia molecules are the ones exerting  the pressure on this side now if we  change the conditions of the pressure of  this reaction by increasing the pressure  we will try and put more pressure on the  container so if we wanted to decrease  the pressure the easiest way to do that  is to reduce the number of particles or  molecules that are pushing against the  side of the container so if we wanted to  decrease the number of molecules the  easiest way to do that is to move the  reaction towards the ammonia side  because when we move towards the ammonia  side there are less molecules present  we've gone from 4 to 2 for each of these  that react so by increasing the pressure  we encourage the reaction in the  direction that will decrease pressure  and that is the one to the side with the  least molecules so increase in the  pressure will promote the reaction that  reduces the number of molecules  so in the harbor prep at the Harbor  process reaction you'll know that the  conditions of the reaction are carried  out at 200 atmospheres  this is 200 times atmospheric pressure  so this is incredibly high pressure it  could be higher but the economics of  this means that if you try to increase  it higher you would not get your money's  worth from the reaction  so simply 200 atmosphere is what it's  limited to our third situation is of  concentration so again if we considered  the harbor process as a reaction of  concentration we have our nitrogen plus  our hydrogen forming our ammonia right  so as this reacts we maybe have only 5%  of our product is ammonia and 95% of our  chemicals as the reactants nitrogen and  hydrogen now if I can reduce the  concentration of ammonia by removing it  this will reduce the concentration of  ammonia on this side if we reduce the  concentration of ammonia on this side  then Lucia Talia's principles will say  that the reaction will compensate this  by moving more of the nitrogen ammonia  over to this side of the equation in  other words by removing this will reduce  that concentration so to increase the  concentration more ammonia or more  nitrogen hydrogen will move to this side  so will promote the forward reaction so  in the case of lechatelier's principle  by removing our product we increase the  forward reaction so remove the product  increases the forward reaction that is  in principle how the three different  changes of conditions  react with Leisha Talia's principle so  for one in summary Alicia Talia's  principles explains how the dynamic or  the equilibrium position in a dynamic  equilibrium can be changed by changing  the conditions and l'Italia Taylor's  chatelier's principle says that it will  change to minimize the effect of any  change that we make in the case of  temperature if we increase the  temperature of the reaction le  chatelier's principle says that we will  then promote the endothermic reaction if  we decrease the temperature will promote  the exothermic reaction in the case of  pressure if we increase the pressure we  will promote the reaction that serves to  reduce the number of molecules and the  case of the harbor process this is the  forward reaction if we reduce the  pressure we will rather promote the  reaction that reduces or increases the  number of molecules site and in the case  of concentration if we remove some of  the concentration of say the product  then the reaction of the forward  direction will be promoted to produce  more of that reaction if for instance we  added more ammonia into this we will  promote the reverse reaction that's a  summary of lechatelier's principle
 

